wer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342023/field-4/e3c3efdc9616633630f5d326ad0e76bb88d19f128e35c58d2b47c5e3732df2a7" :answer/id #uuid "34f0f999-26cc-59a0-aafd-6f599e00afe2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342023/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4ce5cbd-aac1-531c-b8c6-9f57bddf6652" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342023/field-4/be5ec7f4ffd7105834cc564a8dfd68a34c01e1126f59e0d5696d71eaf3f3976d" :answer-field/choices ["q-342023/field-4/977bef0473cf0b2774e405b429991de1e3d542548ba15b4af5a68b95fa47c522" "q-342023/field-4/a85c02d454c5b6cab751380b8c71cfdc093c7da1d3f96e69e31eb1832827f4f9" "q-342023/field-4/be5ec7f4ffd7105834cc564a8dfd68a34c01e1126f59e0d5696d71eaf3f3976d" "q-342023/field-4/e3c3efdc9616633630f5d326ad0e76bb88d19f128e35c58d2b47c5e3732df2a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-342023"] :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=8x$ defined on $[-2,2).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $4$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=4{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n8x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=2$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-2,2).$ \nTherefore, the $4$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}8t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ [4{t}^{2}]_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{2})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{64(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342023/field-2" "q-342023/field-3" "q-342023/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-343245/field-1/a60fe99f250bf026e1c7e8c51252c9d47144bc8d638ae3a550b75955f68ead3c" :answer/id #uuid "11e42a6b-6a26-5d67-b554-a22e67fd5f08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343245/field-1/4f1cc41f8ce9a3ee526cae63a3c1c702a1e792404302f4907ff1b52447a8e94f" :answer/id #uuid "595974f3-6711-56f2-8b18-266d19f195c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343245/field-1/22a4d37d0983a97aab87b95b41a1b53a971b64398f3ee073e7ac40da0c14e126" :answer/id #uuid "71ef2c41-2415-5918-bd81-57604ff12244" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343245/field-1/02ed36065101387bcf15b45d2220bec17bd5ee8469eadf19e4c6f098cb65b0a2" :answer/id #uuid "fe11bf0d-d007-